  "conditionsOfAccess": "Restricted",
  "date": "2010-07-21",
  "dateCreated": "2010-07-21",
  "dateModified": "2010-07-21",
  "description": "Monozygotic (MZ) twins have been widely employed for dissection of the relative contributions of genetics and environment in disease.  \tIn multiple sclerosis (MS), an autoimmune demyelinating disease that commonly causes neurodegeneration and disability in young adults,  \tdisease discordance in MZ twins has been interpreted to indicate environmental importance in pathogenesis. However, genetic and epigenetic  \tdifferences between MZ twins have been described, challenging the accepted experimental paradigm in disambiguating effects of nature and  \tnurture.  Here, we report the genome sequences of one MS-discordant MZ twin pair and messenger RNA (mRNA) transcriptome and epigenome  \tsequences of CD4+ lymphocytes from three MS-discordant, MZ twin pairs. No reproducible differences were detected between co-twins  \tamong ~3.6 million single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) or ~0.2 million insertion-deletion polymorphisms (indels). Nor were any  \treproducible differences observed between siblings of the three twin pairs in HLA haplotypes, confirmed MS-susceptibility SNPs, copy  \tnumber variations, mRNA and genomic SNP and indel genotypes, or expression of ~19,000 genes in CD4+ T cells. Only two to 176 differences  \tin methylation of ~2 million CpG sites were detected between siblings of the three twin pairs, in contrast to ~800 differences in  \tmethylation between T cells of unrelated individuals and several thousand differences between tissues or normal and cancerous tissues.  \tIn the first systematic effort to estimate sequence variation among MZ co-twins, we did not find evidence for genetic, epigenetic or  \ttranscriptome differences that explained disease discordance. These are the first female, twin and autoimmune disease genome sequences  \treported.]]>\n\tThree pairs of adult monozygotic twins who were discordant for MS. Affected individuals fulfilled McDonald criteria for MS diagnosis.  \tLack of affectation in siblings was confirmed through clinical, magnetic resonance brain imaging, and cerebrospinal studies.  Controls  \tfor reduced representation bisulphite sequencing were four tissue samples: one breast cancer, one normal breast tissue, one lung cancer,  \tone normal lung tissue.]]>\n\t",
